NNAAMMEE
       fonttosfnt - Wrap a bitmap font in a sfnt (TrueType) wrapper

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ffoonnttttoossffnntt [ _o_p_t_i_o_n_s ] --oo _f_i_l_e_._t_t_f [ ---- ] _f_o_n_t...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Wrap  a  bitmap  font  or  a set of bitmap fonts in a sfnt (TrueType or
       OpenType) wrapper.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --vv     Be verbose.

       --cc     Do not crop glyphs.  This usually increases file size,  but  may
              sometimes yield a modest decrease in file size for small charac‐
              ter cell fonts (terminal fonts).

       --bb     Write byte-aligned glyph data.  By default,  unaligned  data  is
              written, which yields a smaller file size.

       --rr     Do  not reencode fonts.  By default, fonts are reencoded to Uni‐
              code whenever possible.

       --gg _n   Set the type of scalable glyphs that we write.  If _n  is  0,  no
              scalable  glyphs  are  written;  this is legal but confuses most
              current software.  If _n is 1, a single scalable glyph (the unde‐
              fined glyph) is written; this is recommended, but triggers a bug
              in current versions of FFrreeeeTTyyppee.  If _n is  2  (the  default),  a
              sufficiently  high  number  of  blank  glyphs are written, which
              works with FFrreeeeTTyyppee but increases file size.

       --mm _n   Set the type of scalable metrics that we write.  If _n is  0,  no
              scalable metrics are written, which may or may not be legal.  If
              _n is 1, full metrics for a single glyph are  written,  and  only
              left  sidebearing values are written for the other glyphs.  If _n
              is 2, scalable metrics for all glyphs  are  written,  which  in‐
              creases file size and is not recommended.  The default is 1.

       ----     End of options.

BBUUGGSS
       Some  of the font-level values, notably sub- and superscript positions,
       are dummy values.

